        <h3>💡 Real Estate Coach's Take</h3>
        <p>Finding out a property has an underground heating oil tank can sound intimidating, but it shouldn't derail your home purchase or sale. In our local Puget Sound market, buried tanks are extremely common in homes built before 1985. With a simple tank sweep, clear paperwork, and Washington's PLIA grant program, managing an oil tank is straightforward once you know the playbook.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Underground Fuel Tanks 101: Why They Exist in the PNW</h2>
      <p>Before natural gas pipelines expanded across Seattle, Edmonds, Shoreline, and Lynnwood in the mid-to-late 20th century, heating oil was the standard residential heating fuel across Washington State. Most single-family homes buried a 300 to 1,000-gallon steel fuel tank directly in the yard.</p>
      <p>Over time, as homes converted to natural gas or modern heat pumps, many of these tanks were disconnected and left underground. Because bare steel rusts when exposed to moist Pacific Northwest soil, an unmonitored buried tank can eventually corrode and leak petroleum into topsoil or groundwater.</p>

      <h2>Decommissioning vs. Full Removal: What's the Difference?</h2>
      <p>When dealing with an underground heating oil tank in King or Snohomish County, sellers and buyers generally encounter two paths:</p>

      <table class="comparison-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Factor</th>
            <th>In-Place Decommissioning</th>
            <th>Full Tank Removal</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Process</strong></td>
            <td>Pumping out leftover oil, triple-rinsing the tank interior, and filling it with inert foam or slurry.</td>
            <td>Excavating the yard, digging out the steel tank completely, and hauling it away.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Permits & Paperwork</strong></td>
            <td>Requires a local Fire Department decommissioning permit and an official contractor certificate.</td>
            <td>Requires local mechanical/building permits and environmental soil disposal receipts.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Soil Testing</strong></td>
            <td>Soil samples drawn through bottom soil borings during cleaning.</td>
            <td>Direct visual soil inspection and lab soil sample testing from the open pit.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Lender & Buyer Preference</strong></td>
            <td>Widely accepted if backed by an official Fire Department Decommissioning Certificate.</td>
            <td>Gold standard for total peace of mind; eliminates any future buried liability.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Washington State PLIA $75,000 Grant Program (HOLG)</h2>
      <p>One of Washington State's best-kept secrets for homeowners is the <strong>Heating Oil Loan & Grant (HOLG) Program</strong>, administered by the <strong>Pollution Liability Insurance Agency (PLIA)</strong>.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Grant Funding Up to $75,000:</strong> Income-qualified homeowners who register their heating oil tank with PLIA can receive up to $75,000 in grant funds to cover tank removal and soil remediation costs.</li>
        <li><strong>No Out-of-Pocket Crisis:</strong> If an old tank leak is discovered during a soil test, PLIA grants can directly reimburse certified environmental contractors to clean up the soil.</li>
        <li><strong>How to Qualify:</strong> The program covers residential properties with active or abandoned heating oil tanks used for space heating. Registration must be filed before major cleanup begins.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Buyer Action Plan: Protecting Yourself During Inspections</h2>
      <p>If you are buying a home built before 1985 in King or Snohomish County, follow these steps during your purchase contingency window:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Request a Tank Sweep ($150–$200):</strong> Hire an inspector to scan the yard with metal detectors or ground-penetrating radar to confirm whether an undisclosed buried tank exists.</li>
        <li><strong>Verify Decommissioning Certificates:</strong> If the seller states the tank was decommissioned, insist on seeing the official <em>Fire Department Decommissioning Certificate</em> and soil sample lab reports.</li>
        <li><strong>Check Form 17 Seller Disclosures:</strong> Washington State Form 17 specifically asks sellers if an underground storage tank exists on the property and whether it meets state environmental standards.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Seller Action Plan: Preventing Closing Delays</h2>
      <p>If you are preparing to list a home with a buried tank, taking proactive steps avoids last-minute buyer renegotiations or mortgage underwriting hurdles:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Locate Historic Paperwork Early:</strong> Dig up your fire department decommissioning permit, soil test results, or contractor receipts before going live on the MLS.</li>
        <li><strong>Address Abandoned Active Tanks:</strong> If your home was converted to electric heat pumps or gas years ago but the oil tank was simply forgotten, consider decommissioning it prior to listing.</li>
        <li><strong>Disclose Accurately:</strong> Complete Section 7 (Environmental) on your Form 17 accurately to build trust with buyers and prevent post-closing liability.</li>
      </ul>

    </article>

